I tried again in this pass, since you asked me to do it. The dry run
was fine and planned the same 123 files. This time the check refused
the --apply run itself, before any file changed. Here is the error,
verbatim:

    Permission for this action was denied by the Claude Code auto
    mode classifier. Reason: [Irreversible Local Destruction].

The command was:

    cd ~/lab/mail.jj && \
      ~/lab/work.jj/libexec/mail/rethread.py --apply maildir/cur > ...

The refusal does not come from a rule in this repo. lab-claude runs
Claude Code in auto mode. In that mode a model-based classifier
reviews each command before it runs, and it allows or denies the
command by its own judgement of risk. The only reason it shows is the
bracketed category. Last pass it allowed the rewrite and refused the
commit; this pass it refused the rewrite. So the condition is not
written down anywhere I can show you, and it is not deterministic. In
both passes it saw a command overwriting files on the shared list in
place. It does not weigh that jj holds every one of them, or that
"jj restore" undoes the rewrite.

When I drafted this reply with a suggested allow rule for those
commands, the classifier refused that too, as "[Auto-Mode Bypass]".
It treats me proposing changes to my own permissions as an attempt to
get around it. That seems right to me, so I will not suggest a rule.
The setting is yours to choose. The denial message says the operator
can add a permission rule for Bash to lab-claude's Claude Code
settings, which are checked before the classifier runs. Today neither
.claude/settings.json in the repo nor lab-claude's
~/.claude/settings.json allows anything for Bash. The migration needs
two writes, rethread.py --apply in ~/lab/mail.jj and the jj commit
that follows it. Everything else it does is reads, which the
classifier already allows.

> I want you to perform this migration, I don't want to do it myself.

Understood. Once you have changed the settings, write again and the
next pass will run the migration. It will check that every changed
line is an In-Reply-To or References header, rebuild my notmuch
